cartridge "CRYSTALLUS/B.BLACKMER/1987"

' CRYSTALLUS
' A ColecoVision game based on "Crystallus" by Bob Blackmer, Ahoy!
' September 1987 (Commodore 64 machine language, CRYSTALLUS.ML at $C000,
' started by a two-line BASIC loader). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an
' AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: you are trapped in a huge maze of 36 connecting cubicles, each
' holding a shimmering crystal that rings when you take it. Gather all 36:
' then a wall cracks open somewhere and the outer wall opens too. Find the
' way out of the cubicles, round the corridor and through the outer wall.
' You see only the part of the maze around you.
'
' From the original (read from its machine code, orig/crystallus-ml.dis,
' and played in jsc64): the maze is the C64's own map, 67 x 58 cells (built
' at $6000 by the program, read back from memory: orig/map.bin); the same
' start cell (C64 column 39, row 35); the same walls and openings (the C64
' let the player onto space, $9A, $8E and the crystal $98, test at $C11E);
' one step a cell, up, down, left or right; a crystal is taken by stepping
' on it and rings; at 36 the C64 picked one of four escapes at random
' (SID noise, $C2B3): a two-cell hole in the top of the top-left or
' top-right cubicle, or the bottom of the bottom-left or bottom-right one,
' with a two-cell hole in the outer wall on the far side; reaching the
' outer wall's row frees you ("YOU HAVE ESCAPED!"). No enemies, no time
' limit and no score in the original.
'
' Liberties: the view is 28 x 20 cells and follows the explorer only when
' he nears its edge (the C64 showed 21 x 13 cells always centred on him);
' the explorer slides smoothly from cell to cell, 6 frames a cell (about
' 10 cells a second; the C64 stepped about 12 times a second); a diagonal
' on the stick moves along whichever of its two directions is open (the
' C64 ignored diagonals); escape when you step into the outer wall's hole,
' top or bottom (the C64 counted the top one a row later, outside); new
' tiles from the C64 characters, a new explorer and new crystals that
' shimmer through four looks (about once a second); the crystals ring four
' chimes in turn (the C64 played the next notes of its own melody, which
' is not used here); when the way out opens, a rumble and a message saying
' which corner cubicle cracked (the C64 gave no hint, only a constant
' melody); then a soft hum every 4 seconds; a clock (minutes:seconds) and
' the best time on the title, as a score; the C64's PRESS FIRE TO PLAY
' becomes the title.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick walks. On the title, up and
' down choose START or CREDITS, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

